Upgrade to Symfony 7
********************

.[perex]
Symfony 7.0 was a cleanup: it removed everything Symfony 6 had deprecated, such as the `@Route` annotation, the annotations of SensioFrameworkExtraBundle, `MessageHandlerInterface`, `renderForm()` and the property `$defaultName` of a command. Symfony 6.4 already has all their replacements, so you do most of the upgrade from Symfony 6 to 7 while still on 6.4: DressCode with the package `dresscode/upgrade-symfony` rewrites your code and reports what it cannot rewrite.

According to [UPGRADE-7.0.md |https://github.com/symfony/symfony/blob/7.0/UPGRADE-7.0.md], version 7.0 has the same features as 6.4, only without the deprecated ones. But 7.0 needs PHP 8.2, while 6.4 also runs on PHP 8.1 ([Symfony 7.0 |https://symfony.com/releases/7.0]).


What It Looks Like
==================

This controller was written for Symfony 6.4, and `composer.json` has the Symfony packages at `6.4.*`. It uses three things that Symfony 7.0 removed:

```php .[before]
use Symfony\Component\Routing\Annotation\Route;


class OrderController extends AbstractController
{
	/** @Route("/orders/{id}", name="order_show") */
	public function show(Request $request, int $id): Response
	{
		$form = $this->createForm(OrderType::class);
		return $this->renderForm('order/show.html.twig', [
			'form' => $form,
			'format' => $request->getContentType(),
		]);
	}
}
```

After `dresscode upgrade today`, still on Symfony 6.4:

```php .[after]
use Symfony\Component\Routing\Attribute\Route;


class OrderController extends AbstractController
{
	#[Route('/orders/{id}', name: 'order_show')]
	public function show(Request $request, int $id): Response
	{
		$form = $this->createForm(OrderType::class);
		return $this->render('order/show.html.twig', [
			'form' => $form,
			'format' => $request->getContentTypeFormat(),
		]);
	}
}
```

The annotation became an attribute of the same name, imported from the namespace `Attribute` instead of `Annotation`. `renderForm()` became `render()`, which takes the form as it is, and `getContentType()` became `getContentTypeFormat()`. The code still runs on 6.4. After the versions in `composer.json` were raised to `7.0.*`, a second run found nothing more to rewrite in this controller.


Step by Step
============

The Symfony packages are raised all at once, so you raise them in `composer.json` yourself and `dresscode upgrade today` rewrites the code. DressCode needs PHP 8.4. If your application runs on an older PHP, install DressCode globally, with PHPStan and the data for Symfony:

```shell
composer global require dresscode/dresscode dresscode/upgrade-symfony phpstan/phpstan
```

On PHP 8.4 or newer, it can be a development dependency of the project, as [Installation |upgrading-symfony#Installation] shows.

1. Start from Symfony 6.4 with a clean working tree. Run `dresscode upgrade today`, and once more if it advises you to. It rewrites what Symfony 6.1 to 6.4 deprecated, because the replacements already exist in 6.4.
2. Go through what it reports, run your tests and PHPStan, and commit.
3. Raise the Symfony packages in `composer.json` to `7.0.*`, or to a later 7.x, and `require.php` to at least 8.2. With Symfony Flex, raise `extra.symfony.require` too. Then run `composer update "symfony/*" -W --prefer-lowest`.
4. Run `dresscode upgrade today` again. It finds what is left after raising the versions, and on a later 7.x also what that version deprecated. Then run the tests, and commit.

.[warning]
DressCode reads the version of each package from its constraint in `composer.json`. If a Symfony package has only `*` there and leaves the version to `extra.symfony.require`, DressCode does not see the lower bound and only reports the changes of that package. Write the version into the constraint, such as `6.4.*`.

[Upgrading Code |upgrade#One Major Version at a Time, by Hand] explains why `--prefer-lowest` and why one major version at a time.


SensioFrameworkExtraBundle Annotations
======================================

SensioFrameworkExtraBundle is abandoned, and its last version 6.2.10 allows `symfony/framework-bundle` only up to 6.x, as [Packagist |https://packagist.org/packages/sensio/framework-extra-bundle] shows. Since Symfony 6.2, the framework has its own attributes for these annotations, and DressCode writes them in place of the annotations:

```php .[before]
use Sensio\Bundle\FrameworkExtraBundle\Configuration\Cache;
use Sensio\Bundle\FrameworkExtraBundle\Configuration\IsGranted;
use Sensio\Bundle\FrameworkExtraBundle\Configuration\Template;
use Symfony\Bundle\FrameworkBundle\Controller\AbstractController;


class ProductController extends AbstractController
{
	/**
	 * @IsGranted("ROLE_EDITOR")
	 * @Cache(maxage=3600, public=true)
	 * @Template("product/list.html.twig")
	 */
	public function list(): array
	{
		return ['products' => []];
	}
}
```

```php .[after]
use Sensio\Bundle\FrameworkExtraBundle\Configuration\Cache;
use Sensio\Bundle\FrameworkExtraBundle\Configuration\IsGranted;
use Sensio\Bundle\FrameworkExtraBundle\Configuration\Template;
use Symfony\Bundle\FrameworkBundle\Controller\AbstractController;


class ProductController extends AbstractController
{
	#[\Symfony\Component\Security\Http\Attribute\IsGranted('ROLE_EDITOR')]
	#[\Symfony\Component\HttpKernel\Attribute\Cache(maxage: 3600, public: true)]
	#[\Symfony\Bridge\Twig\Attribute\Template('product/list.html.twig')]
	public function list(): array
	{
		return ['products' => []];
	}
}
```

The imports of the bundle stay in the file, so the new attributes are written with their fully qualified names, which cannot clash with them. Delete the imports when you remove the bundle. The annotation `@Security`, whose expression only calls `is_granted()`, becomes `#[IsGranted]` too, as the page [Symfony |upgrading-symfony] shows.


What Gets Rewritten
===================

The data of the package `dresscode/upgrade-symfony` hold 135 changes that Symfony deprecated in versions 6.1 to 6.4 and removed in 7.0. DressCode rewrites 98 of them by itself and reports the other 37. The table shows examples, each verified by a run of DressCode. The complete list is in the data of the package, one file per component, for example [console.neon |https://github.com/dg/dresscode-upgrade-symfony/blob/master/upgrading/console.neon].

| old API | new API | since |
|---|---|---|
| `protected static $defaultName = 'app:import';` | `#[AsCommand(name: 'app:import')]` on the class | `symfony/console` 6.1 |
| `/** @Route("/orders/{id}") */` from `Routing\Annotation` | `#[Route('/orders/{id}')]` from `Routing\Attribute` | `symfony/routing` 6.4 |
| `class OrderPlacedHandler implements MessageHandlerInterface` | `#[AsMessageHandler] class OrderPlacedHandler` | `symfony/messenger` 6.2 |
| `Symfony\Component\Security\Core\Security` | `Symfony\Bundle\SecurityBundle\Security` | `symfony/security-bundle` 6.2 |
| `$this->renderForm($view, ['form' => $form])` | `$this->render($view, ['form' => $form])` | `symfony/framework-bundle` 6.2 |
| `$request->getContentType()` | `$request->getContentTypeFormat()` | `symfony/http-foundation` 6.2 |
| `$response->setEtag()` | `$response->setEtag(null)` | `symfony/http-foundation` 6.2 |
| `/** @required */` | `#[Required]` | `symfony/dependency-injection` 6.3 |
| `@Assert\Regex("/^\w+$/")` | `#[Assert\Regex('/^\w+$/')]` | `symfony/validator` 6.4 |
| `$e->getNestedExceptions()` | `\array_values($e->getWrappedExceptions())` | `symfony/messenger` 6.4 |
| `Symfony\Component\HttpKernel\UriSigner` | `Symfony\Component\HttpFoundation\UriSigner` | `symfony/http-kernel` 6.4 |
| `SendinblueApiTransport` | `BrevoApiTransport` | `symfony/brevo-mailer` 6.4 |

The data have no section for 7.0, because 7.0 brought the same features as 6.4 and only removed what was deprecated. What Symfony 6.0 changed, such as `MASTER_REQUEST` renamed to `MAIN_REQUEST`, belongs to the move from Symfony 5 to 6, and the package covers it as well.


What Gets Reported
==================

Where the replacement is used differently, or there is none, DressCode reports the place, leaves the code alone and says what to write instead. A class that takes services from the container is a typical case:

```
Class `Symfony\Component\DependencyInjection\ContainerAwareInterface` is deprecated: pass the services the class needs to its constructor, or implement `Symfony\Contracts\Service\ServiceSubscriberInterface` to fetch them lazily, since symfony/dependency-injection 6.4.
```

| old API | what to write instead | since |
|---|---|---|
| `ContainerAwareInterface`, `ContainerAwareTrait` | the services in the constructor, or `ServiceSubscriberInterface` to fetch them lazily | `symfony/dependency-injection` 6.4 |
| `CacheableSupportsMethodInterface` | `getSupportedTypes()` of the normalizer, which says for each type whether `supports*()` may be cached | `symfony/serializer` 6.3 |
| `PostSubmitEvent::setData()` | `setData()` of `PreSubmitEvent` or `SubmitEvent`, in a listener that runs before the form is submitted | `symfony/form` 6.4 |
| `Symfony\Bridge\Doctrine\Logger\DbalLogger` | a DBAL middleware such as `Doctrine\DBAL\Logging\Middleware` | `symfony/doctrine-bridge` 6.4 |
| `StopWorkerOnSignalsListener` | nothing, `messenger:consume` stops the worker on `SIGTERM` and `SIGINT` by itself; remove the listener | `symfony/messenger` 6.4 |
| `Kernel::stripComments()` | no replacement; copy the method into the project | `symfony/http-kernel` 6.4 |

Confirm such a place with the comment `// dresscode:ignore <decision> -- <reason>`, or rewrite it; [Manual Tasks Along the Way |upgrade-plan#Manual Tasks Along the Way] has the details.

The data do not capture changes in YAML configuration, in Twig templates or in behavior. Symfony 7.0 also added native return types to many methods ([Symfony 7.0 type declarations |https://symfony.com/blog/symfony-7-0-type-declarations]), and a method of yours that overrides one of them needs the same type; the page [Symfony |upgrading-symfony] says how DressCode writes such signatures.


